WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.700
When we, humans look at an image, or a piece
of video footage, we understand the geometry

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.749
of the objects in there so well, that if we
have the time and patience, we could draw

00:00:14.749 --> 00:00:20.030
a depth map that describes the distance of
each object from the camera.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:21.880
This goes without saying.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.580
However, what does not go without saying,
is that if we could teach computers to do

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.590
the same, we could do incredible things.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:34.500
For instance, this learning-based technique
creates real-time defocus effects for virtual

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:42.059
reality and computer games, and this one performs
this Ken Burns effect in 3D, or in other words,

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:47.969
zoom and pan around in a photograph, but,
with a beautiful twist, because in the meantime,

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:50.899
it also reveals the depth of the image.

00:00:50.899 --> 00:00:56.800
With this data, we can even try to teach self-driving
cars about depth perception to enhance their

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.480
ability to navigate around safely.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:05.670
However, if you look here, you see two key
problems: one, it is a little blurry, and

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:10.790
there are lots of fine details that it couldn’t
resolve, and, it is flickering.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:15.340
In other words, there are abrupt changes from
one image to the next one, which shouldn’t

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.240
be there as the objects in the video feed
are moving smoothly.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.840
Smooth motion should mean smooth depth maps,
and it is getting there, but it still is not

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:26.900
the case here.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:32.200
So, I wonder, if we could teach a machine
to perform this task better?

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.500
And more importantly, what new wondrous things
can we do if we pull this off?

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.010
This new technique is called Consistent Video
Depth Estimation, and it promises smooth and

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:48.550
detailed depth maps that are of much higher
quality than what previous works offer.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:54.500
And, now, hold on to your papers, because
finally, these maps contain enough detail

00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:00.850
to open up the possibility of adding new objects
to the scene, or even flood the room with

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.700
water, or add many other, really cool video
effects.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.590
All of these will take the geometry of the
existing real-world objects, for instance,

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.650
cats into consideration.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.040
Very cool!

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.810
The reason why we need such a consistent technique
to pull this off, is because if we have this

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:26.230
flickering in time that we’ve seen here,
then, the depth of different objects suddenly

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:30.900
bounces around over time, even for a stationary
object.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.930
This means, that in one frame, the ball would
be in front of the person, when in the next

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.700
one, it would suddenly think that it has to
put the ball behind them, and then, in the

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:47.370
next one, front again, creating a not only
jarring, but quite unconvincing animation.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:51.540
What is really remarkable is that due to the
consistency of the technique, none of that

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:52.930
happens here.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.180
Love it!

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.349
Here are some more results where you can see
that the outlines of the objects in the depth

00:02:58.349 --> 00:03:05.319
map are really crisp, and follow the changes
really well over time.

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:10.580
The snowing example here is one of my favorites,
and it is really convincing.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:15.680
However, there are still a few spots where
we can find some visual artifacts.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:21.130
For instance, as the subject is waving, there
is lots of fine, high-frequency data around

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:25.810
the fingers there, and if you look at the
region behind the head closely, you find some

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:31.770
more issues, or you can find that some balls
are flickering on the table as we move the

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.880
camera around.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:39.350
Compare that to previous methods that could
not do nearly as good as this, and now, we

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.510
have something that is quite satisfactory.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:46.910
I can only imagine how good this will get
to more papers down the line.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:51.730
And for the meantime, we’ll be able to run
these amazing effects even without having

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:56.680
a real depth camera.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:27.919
What a

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:40.160
time

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:48.480
to be alive!

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:58.050
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

